WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.835
-I have so much to talk to you
about -- your show, your movie.

00:00:01.835 --> 00:00:03.436 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I also want
to congratulate you.

00:00:03.436 --> 00:00:04.838 align:start size:94% position:6%
You got engaged.
-I did.

00:00:04.838 --> 00:00:06.473 align:start size:94% position:6%
Congratulations.
-Thank you.

00:00:06.473 --> 00:00:07.207 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:07.207 --> 00:00:09.409
I popped the question, man.
-You popped the question.

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:11.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thanksgiving, is that right?
-I did on Thanksgiving.

00:00:11.711 --> 00:00:13.079 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Gotcha.
-It was kind of cheesy but --

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.281
-I think that's all right.
-It has special place in

00:00:15.281 --> 00:00:17.817 align:start size:94% position:6%
my heart, that holiday now.
-I believe it.

00:00:17.817 --> 00:00:19.085 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah.
-It was good.

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:20.387 align:start size:94% position:6%
-So, you bought
a ring and everything?

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:22.555 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Bought a ring.
-Did you get a ring back?

00:00:22.555 --> 00:00:24.591
-No.
Because that's the weird thing,

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:26.693 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, lesbian couples --
you got the masculine,

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.228
you got the feminine.
It's like, "I don't want, like,

00:00:28.228 --> 00:00:30.330 size:81% position:41%
a ring, that's, like,
a little too feminine.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:31.431 line:92%
So like, give me some sneakers."

00:00:31.431 --> 00:00:33.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, okay.
-So she got me some.

00:00:33.099 --> 00:00:35.702
We were in Tokyo and she got me
some sneakers that you can

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.570 align:start size:94% position:6%
only get in Tokyo.
So I can literally be like,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:40.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
"Y'all ain't got these, man."
[ Laughter ]

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:43.043 size:81% position:41%
You know, you gotta go
to Tokyo to get these.

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:43.610 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-That's great.

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.112 align:start size:94% position:6%
You mentioned that you have
a connection with Thanksgiving

00:00:46.112 --> 00:00:49.115
forever now. You wrote --
won an Emmy for an episode

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
of "Master of None" you wrote,
which is like your

00:00:51.251 --> 00:00:53.386 line:92%
personal story of coming out.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:56.589 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Which is not fun
for people, period.

00:00:56.589 --> 00:01:00.493 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The coming-out story?
-Coming out is not fun, ever.

00:01:00.493 --> 00:01:03.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
But when you're far away
from it, which you've done it,

00:01:03.296 --> 00:01:04.397 align:start size:94% position:6%
then you can make jokes
and have fun

00:01:04.397 --> 00:01:06.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
and call Angela Bassett
to come play a sweet version

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:09.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
of your mother.
You know, and have Kim Whitley

00:01:09.936 --> 00:01:11.471 align:start size:94% position:6%
play a nice aunty.
You know, it's cool.

00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:14.007 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, like, during the time,
it's, like, horrifying.

00:01:14.007 --> 00:01:16.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's, like, the worst.
-Was it something that you --

00:01:16.976 --> 00:01:19.312
How did it come about that it
became an episode of the show?

00:01:19.312 --> 00:01:22.715 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, honestly, like,
Alan Yang, who is co-creator

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.384 align:start size:94% position:6%
of "Master of None,"
just asked me.

00:01:24.384 --> 00:01:25.452 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like,
"How did you come out?"

00:01:25.452 --> 00:01:27.921
And I literally did a one-woman
show of the episode that

00:01:27.921 --> 00:01:29.989 align:start size:94% position:6%
you guys now know is
Thanksgiving, and they were

00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:32.692
like, "That's an episode, that's
an episode of 'Master of None.'"

00:01:32.692 --> 00:01:35.628
I was like, "Really? I don't
think it's that interesting."

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.263 align:start size:94% position:6%
But they were like,
"No, you got to write it."

00:01:37.263 --> 00:01:38.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
"You going to get it done."
And I was like,

00:01:38.531 --> 00:01:41.301
"I got too much to do, man."
I got to do the Spielberg movie.

00:01:41.301 --> 00:01:42.902 size:84% position:42%
I'm still trying to get
"The Chi" green=lit.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.238
And Aziz was just like, "Look,
I can't write that by myself."

00:01:45.238 --> 00:01:49.042
That's a bad Aziz impersonation.
I don't want to do him dirty.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.243 size:78% position:39% line:92%
And go up a couple --

00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:51.177 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:51.177 --> 00:01:53.446
But, he was like, "You know,
I can't write this by myself."

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.447 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like,
"This is like your story.

00:01:54.447 --> 00:01:56.382
I need you to help me tell it."
I'm so grateful that he

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.017 size:78% position:39%
made me do it because
it changed my life.

00:01:58.017 --> 00:01:59.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
-It was a fantastic episode
of television.

00:01:59.252 --> 00:02:00.353 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you so much.
-Congratulations.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:02.122 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:02.122 --> 00:02:06.359
-How -- because you then had
to write it in London while

00:02:06.359 --> 00:02:07.694 align:start size:94% position:6%
you were working on
"Ready Player One."

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
How was your --
Steven Spielberg?

00:02:09.262 --> 00:02:11.598 align:start size:94% position:6%
What's that like,
to be around Steven Spielberg?

00:02:11.598 --> 00:02:14.267
-Well, Steven Spielberg is
the Jewish father you didn't

00:02:14.267 --> 00:02:16.402 align:start size:94% position:6%
know you needed.
-Yeah, okay.

00:02:16.402 --> 00:02:18.605 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And he's amazing.
Like, I always say he's

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.373 size:84% position:42%
a giant that doesn't
make people feel small.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.509 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh. That's a great way
of putting it, yeah.

00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:23.643 align:start size:94% position:6%
-He's so cool.
He let's you play,

00:02:23.643 --> 00:02:25.912
he let's you do your thing.
And even when it's your fault

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:27.580 size:88% position:44%
when you mess up a take,
he don't make you think

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
it's your fault.
He's like, "That's my bad,

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:32.585 align:start size:94% position:6%
camera guy, bad camera guy!
You messed up!"

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:33.953 align:start size:94% position:6%
He's like,
"Now we got to do it again.

00:02:33.953 --> 00:02:36.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
Okay, Lena, I apologize."
Like, he's so great.

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:39.893
Like, he just always makes you
feel right at home on the set.

00:02:39.893 --> 00:02:41.728
-I know it just premiered
at South by Southwest.

00:02:41.728 --> 00:02:43.396 align:start size:94% position:6%
I know people loved it,
great response.

00:02:43.396 --> 00:02:47.167
For those who don't know, what
is the premise of the movie?

00:02:47.167 --> 00:02:50.370
Because I heard it's described
as sort of a nerdgasm for all

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
the pop culture references
in this film.

00:02:52.839 --> 00:02:54.741 line:92%
-Yes, that's a fair assessment.

00:02:54.741 --> 00:02:58.278
But it's also about -- like,
imagine social media times,

00:02:58.278 --> 00:03:00.680
like, 200.
So it's like, you can literally

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:03.449
go to another planet and be
with your avatar picture.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:05.718
If you want to think you're
Tupac, you can go be Tupac.

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.321
You know what I'm saying?
Muhammad Ali -- It's really

00:03:08.321 --> 00:03:10.290 size:81% position:41%
its own universe and
it's called the Oasis.

00:03:10.290 --> 00:03:12.759 align:start size:94% position:6%
And people get to be whoever
they want in the Oasis.

00:03:12.759 --> 00:03:14.661 align:start size:94% position:6%
But what happens is there's
this evil guy played

00:03:14.661 --> 00:03:18.631
by Ben Mendelsohn who wants
to charge people to come

00:03:18.631 --> 00:03:20.567 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the Oasis.
And the cool thing about

00:03:20.567 --> 00:03:22.902
Ty's character and my character,
Olivia's character, all of us,

00:03:22.902 --> 00:03:26.139
we want the Oasis free and cool
and a place people can chill.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:29.042
But it's also about being
too caught up in escapism,

00:03:29.042 --> 00:03:30.143 align:start size:94% position:6%
which I think to me is
a great metaphor.

00:03:30.143 --> 00:03:32.312
Because you know, a lot of us
are on social media more than

00:03:32.312 --> 00:03:34.247 line:92%
we are involved in our lives.

00:03:34.247 --> 00:03:35.315 align:start size:94% position:6%
And that, to me, is
at the root of it.

00:03:35.315 --> 00:03:38.918
It's all about escapism is cool,
but we got to appreciate

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:40.620 line:92%
real life, which is really nice.

